## Slide Accessibility Note
- **Images:** no images; diagrams are HTML text with labels.
- **Speaker Notes:** These slides follow the book chapter Peer Review And
  Revision, which has every step, the Issue template, and the rubric.

## Today

::: {.diagram-flow aria-label="Today's workflow: clone your reviewee's repository fresh, render it without edits, write the review from the template, then file it as an Issue on their repository"}
<div class="diagram-node">Clone</div>
<div class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">-&gt;</div>
<div class="diagram-node">Render</div>
<div class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">-&gt;</div>
<div class="diagram-node">Write</div>
<div class="diagram-arrow" aria-hidden="true">-&gt;</div>
<div class="diagram-node">File Issue</div>
:::

<p class="diagram-caption">No new R today. The review is the work.</p>

- You review **your reviewee**. Someone else is reviewing **you**, right now
- The list is on Canvas
- Everything is in the book: **Peer Review And Revision**

## Why Review?

- A **fresh clone** has only what was pushed. It is the honest test of
  "reproducible"
- Reading someone else's analysis closely is how you see the gaps in
  your own
- Your reviewer **never grades you**. You are graded on the review you
  **give** (60) and how you **respond** (40)

